  "problem": "Let\n$C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\nThen, the dimensions of $CD$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We notice that $C$ is a $4\\times 1$ matrix and $D$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $C$ and $1$ row in $D.$ Since the number of columns in $C$ equals the number of rows in $D,$ the matrices $C$ and $D$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $CD$ has dimensions $4\\times 3:$\n\n$$\n{C}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{D}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3}={CD}_{4\\times 3}\n$$",
